                <pb facs="0169" n="135" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v15p1/pg135"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi></head>
                <p>The United Nations Command, as the official military history of the Korean war
                    points out, was seriously handicapped in its discussions on item 4 at Panmunjom
                    by ignorance of the actual number of prisoners who would resist repatriation. In
                    mid-February, the Chief of the UNC Staff
                    estimated that approximately 16,000 of the 132,000 military prisoners and 30,000
                    of the 37,000 civilian internees held by the United Nations would resist
                    repatriation; see Hermes, <hi rendition="#italic">Truce Tent and Fighting
                        Front</hi>, pages 168–169. On March 17, Ridgway reported to the Joint Chiefs of Staff that the latest,
                    although highly conjectural, estimate was that 73,000 persons including civilian
                    internees, might not elect to be <pb facs="0170" n="136" xml:id="pg136"/>
                    returned; see Ridgway to JCS, telegram CX 65424, March 17, <ref
                        target="#pg91">page 91</ref>. When the UNC
                    and Communist staff officers met in executive session at Panmunjom to discuss
                    the prisoner of war question, Colonel
                        Hickman of the UNC Staff observed
                    to his Communist counterpart on April 1 that possibly 116,000 persons would
                    indicate “the magnitude of the exchange” although the figure was by no means
                    definite. On the next day, the Communists suggested that debate on the POW question be deferred while both sides checked
                    their lists and determined a “round figure” of prisoners to be exchanged. A
                    summary of these executive sessions is in telegrams C 66228 and C 66285,
                        Ridgway to JCS, April 1 and 2 (FE files, lot 55
                    D 128, tabs 7 and 9, respectively).</p>
            </div>
